# The composed remainder, pivoted through the parts, beside the one layers/remainder.sqlc derives
# from the composed totals. One row per composed layer that owes an exact remainder.
#
# Not `n_composed - d_composed`
#   `r_composed = F Phi r_parts - e_n + e_d`, and the point is that it is not
#   `n_composed - d_composed`. One conversion factor multiplies both a part's nameplate and its
#   demand, so the two converted totals are correlated and differencing them counts that factor's
#   spread twice. Converting each part's own remainder puts one occurrence of the factor on each
#   term and the correlation is gone. composition/settled_remainders.sqlc is that pivot, one row
#   per settled node with the path's factors already multiplied in; this is the sum over it plus
#   the correction.
#
# Both eliminations appear, with opposite signs
#   Removing double-counted demand raises the remainder; removing double-counted nameplate lowers
#   it. An identity written in prose and evaluated nowhere can lose a whole term without anything
#   noticing, and this one lost `+ e_d` while it lived only in a document. This relation is what
#   evaluates it, and `examples/matrices/main.rs` §4 is where the identity is written down.
#
# Which corner an elimination takes
#   An elimination is counted in one quantity, so it moves with that quantity: `e_n` is at the
#   nameplate's low where the nameplate is low, `e_d` at the demand's high where the demand is
#   high. Which end of its own range that is, the quantity's sum rule has already decided:
#   bound by bound it is low with low, and where the sum inverted and took the crossed pairing it
#   is low with high. eliminations/paired.sqlc decides which, for the eliminations filed here and,
#   through composition/carried_eliminations.sqlc, for those at the nodes a walk passes, so
#   `n_at_low`, `n_at_high`, `d_at_low` and `d_at_high` are each elimination read at its own
#   quantity's corners. Then the corner of `r` decides which end of each quantity is in play:
#
#     no factor spread          the driver is `d`, and `r = n - d` falls as `d` rises, so
#                               `r_low` takes the nameplate at its low and the demand at its high
#     spread factor, r > 0      both `n` and `d` scale with the factor, so `r` rises with it,
#                               and `r_low` takes both quantities at their low
#
#   With no spread this makes the pivot exactly `n_composed - d_composed` crossed, wherever the
#   composed layer's filed totals are their sums. Proof: `src/proofs/README.md`, entry
#   `composed_remainder`.
#
# The sign of the sum is not the discriminator
#   It only looks like one. It agrees with the driver test on every layer whose `r` keeps one
#   sign, and disagrees where `r` straddles zero, because there the sign changes between the two
#   corners while the driver does not. `every-local-part/both-views` is that layer: two identical
#   parts and eliminations equal to one whole copy of each, so removing one copy from two must
#   leave exactly one copy. Read with the sign test its high comes out 600 too large, which is
#   `e_d`'s own width.
#
# Four layers that tell the rules apart
#   The figure each must land on:
#     merge-group-composition/labour            driver d, r < 0        (-3.0, -1.5, -0.4)
#     merge-holding-composition/compute         driver phi, r > 0      (1414.0, 2857.0, 4085.6)
#     every-local-part/both-views               driver d, r straddles  (-240, 160, 360)
#     every-inverting-elimination/shift-capacity  nameplate crossed    (0, 4, 7)
#
# One read of each elimination relation, pivoted
#   Both quantities' eliminations come from one copy of eliminations/paired.sqlc and one of
#   composition/carried_eliminations.sqlc, each keyed by quantity and turned into columns with
#   `FILTER`. A join per quantity asked the same relation twice and planned it twice, and it sits
#   under every rule that reads a remainder.
#
# The zero fill is sound only inside the owed population
#   The `coalesce(..., 0)` is sound only because composition/owed_remainder.sqlc is inner joined
#   first. Inside that population an absent elimination means the composer looked and found no
#   double counting, which is the number zero. Outside it the same absence means nobody looked,
#   and filling it would put a claim in the checker the document declined to make. The population
#   excludes a layer whose walk passes a node whose own sum is suspended, which is what keeps the
#   carried terms numbers too.
#
# A filed remainder that adds `e_d` straight
#   Where a filed remainder adds `e_d` straight it sits one elimination-width below this figure,
#   which is the right rule for an addition applied to an operand that crosses. Observation 13
#   prints this relation beside the differenced one. Two figures in one document cannot both be
#   right where an elimination has spread, and which of them moves is a filer's call rather than
#   this relation's.
#
# The two eliminations are not independent
#   Each is read at the corner of `r` through the quantity it was counted in, so `e_n` and `e_d`
#   move with the same scenario. Read as independent, as interval arithmetic with no correlation
#   would, `both-views` comes out at a low of -840: a week in which the double-counted demand is at
#   its least while the double-counted nameplate is at its most, which the filing's own figures do
#   not admit. That independent reading is kept for one case only: where the paired reading
#   inverts, it is the widest ordered claim the terms allow, and the pivot falls back to it.
-- composition/settled_remainders.sqlc summed over the settled frontier, less eliminations/paired.sqlc at their own corners.
SELECT y.composition, y.composed_layer,
       CASE WHEN y.paired_low <= y.pivoted_mode AND y.pivoted_mode <= y.paired_high
            THEN y.paired_low  ELSE y.r_low  - y.n_high + y.d_low  END AS pivoted_low,
       y.pivoted_mode,
       CASE WHEN y.paired_low <= y.pivoted_mode AND y.pivoted_mode <= y.paired_high
            THEN y.paired_high ELSE y.r_high - y.n_low  + y.d_high END AS pivoted_high,
       y.derived_low, y.derived_mode, y.derived_high, y.derived_fit, y.unit, y.parts, y.spread
FROM (
    SELECT x.*,
           x.r_low  - x.n_at_low
             + CASE WHEN x.spread AND x.r_low >= 0 THEN x.d_at_low  ELSE x.d_at_high END AS paired_low,
           x.r_mode - x.n_mode + x.d_mode                                            AS pivoted_mode,
           x.r_high - x.n_at_high
             + CASE WHEN x.spread AND x.r_low >= 0 THEN x.d_at_high ELSE x.d_at_low  END AS paired_high,
           least(x.n_at_low, x.n_at_high)    AS n_low,
           greatest(x.n_at_low, x.n_at_high) AS n_high,
           least(x.d_at_low, x.d_at_high)    AS d_low,
           greatest(x.d_at_low, x.d_at_high) AS d_high
    FROM (
        SELECT c.composition, c.composed_layer,
               sum(c.r_low)  AS r_low,
               sum(c.r_mode) AS r_mode,
               sum(c.r_high) AS r_high,
               bool_or(c.spread_factor) AS spread,
               coalesce(max(e.n_at_low),  0) + coalesce(max(k.n_low),  0) AS n_at_low,
               coalesce(max(e.n_mode),    0) + coalesce(max(k.n_mode), 0) AS n_mode,
               coalesce(max(e.n_at_high), 0) + coalesce(max(k.n_high), 0) AS n_at_high,
               coalesce(max(e.d_at_low),  0) + coalesce(max(k.d_low),  0) AS d_at_low,
               coalesce(max(e.d_mode),    0) + coalesce(max(k.d_mode), 0) AS d_mode,
               coalesce(max(e.d_at_high), 0) + coalesce(max(k.d_high), 0) AS d_at_high,
               max(d.r_low)  AS derived_low,
               max(d.r_mode) AS derived_mode,
               max(d.r_high) AS derived_high,
               max(d.derived_fit) AS derived_fit,
               max(d.unit)   AS unit,
               count(*)      AS parts
        FROM      (
            :compose(composition/settled_remainders.sqlc)
        ) c
        JOIN      (
            :compose(composition/owed_remainder.sqlc)
        ) o  ON o.filing = c.composition AND o.layer = c.composed_layer
        JOIN      (
            :compose(layers/differenced_remainder.sqlc)
        ) d  ON d.filing = c.composition AND d.layer = c.composed_layer
        LEFT JOIN (
            SELECT p.composition, p.composed_layer,
                   max(p.at_low)  FILTER (WHERE p.quantity = 'nameplate') AS n_at_low,
                   max(p.mode)    FILTER (WHERE p.quantity = 'nameplate') AS n_mode,
                   max(p.at_high) FILTER (WHERE p.quantity = 'nameplate') AS n_at_high,
                   max(p.at_low)  FILTER (WHERE p.quantity = 'demand')    AS d_at_low,
                   max(p.mode)    FILTER (WHERE p.quantity = 'demand')    AS d_mode,
                   max(p.at_high) FILTER (WHERE p.quantity = 'demand')    AS d_at_high
            FROM (
                :compose(eliminations/paired.sqlc)
            ) p
            GROUP BY p.composition, p.composed_layer
        ) e ON e.composition = c.composition AND e.composed_layer = c.composed_layer
        LEFT JOIN (
            SELECT w.composition, w.composed_layer,
                   max(w.e_low)  FILTER (WHERE w.quantity = 'nameplate') AS n_low,
                   max(w.e_mode) FILTER (WHERE w.quantity = 'nameplate') AS n_mode,
                   max(w.e_high) FILTER (WHERE w.quantity = 'nameplate') AS n_high,
                   max(w.e_low)  FILTER (WHERE w.quantity = 'demand')    AS d_low,
                   max(w.e_mode) FILTER (WHERE w.quantity = 'demand')    AS d_mode,
                   max(w.e_high) FILTER (WHERE w.quantity = 'demand')    AS d_high
            FROM (
                :compose(composition/carried_eliminations.sqlc)
            ) w
            GROUP BY w.composition, w.composed_layer
        ) k ON k.composition = c.composition AND k.composed_layer = c.composed_layer
        GROUP BY c.composition, c.composed_layer
    ) x
) y
